--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. STEP 1 -- THE CONTROL: A NOTICE IS NOT STORED UNDER THE DEFAULT FILTER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DESIGNED: with the default `warnings` filter in force, generate a notice-level event. It
must appear in the buffered log and must NOT appear in the permanent log.

THE STIMULUS. The DUT logs every CLI command it is given, as
`user.notice awplus IMISH[<pid>]: [manager@ttyS0]<the command text>`. So typing a
harmless command carrying a unique marker produces a notice-level message with known
text, on demand, with no traffic and no port flap. (Established in T33401 section 3.)

  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  awplus# show log | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:35:35 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:36:19 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:36:24 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
      -- present in the BUFFERED log, three times (two marker commands and the read's own
         echo)

  awplus# show log permanent | include CKT33402-NOTICE-1
      -- NO OUTPUT. Not present in the PERMANENT log.

  And it was still absent at the filesystem level at the end of the case, checked
  directly rather than inferred (section 10):
    grep -c CKT33402-NOTICE-1 /flash/log/messages /flash/log/messages.1 /flash/log/messages.2
      /flash/log/messages:0
      /flash/log/messages.1:0
      /flash/log/messages.2:0

  STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS -- the permanent log is SELECTIVE. A notice-level event is
  accepted by the buffered log and refused by the permanent log, because the permanent
  filter is `warnings`. Everything stored later in this case is stored because the filter
  was deliberately changed, not because the device keeps everything.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. STEP 2 -- RAISE THE FILTER AND WRITE THREE MARKERS, ONE OF THEM AFTER `write`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  awplus# configure terminal
  awplus(config)# log permanent level informational
  awplus(config)# end
  awplus# show running-config | include log
  log permanent level informational
  aaa authentication login default local

  awplus# show log config          (Permanent log section after the change)
    Permanent log:
      Status ......... enabled
      Maximum size ... 50kb
      Filters:
     *1   Level ...... warnings      Program .... any   Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any           Type ....... include
      2   Level ...... informational Program .... any   Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any           Type ....... include
      Statistics ..... 1777 messages received, 43 accepted, 0 excluded

>>> OBSERVATION O-5. `log permanent level informational` does NOT change the existing
>>> filter -- it APPENDS a second one. The default `*1 Level warnings` is still there and
>>> still marked with the asterisk; the new `2 Level informational` sits beside it. Both
>>> are `Type ... include`, so the effect is the UNION, and informational-and-above now
>>> pass. This is the same mechanism T33401 found behind `log host <ip> facility` (its
>>> FINDING F-1): on this platform every `log <destination> <criterion>` sub-command is a
>>> FILTER BUILDER, not a property setter. Worth knowing before writing a case that
>>> expects a setting to be replaced, and worth knowing when removing one.

THE THREE MARKERS, and why the second one matters

  13:40:17  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-1      <- BEFORE the write
  13:40:19  awplus# write
            Building configuration...
            [OK]
  13:40:20  awplus# show startup-config | include log
            log permanent level informational
            aaa authentication login default local
  13:40:21  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-2      <- AFTER the write
  13:41:34  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-3      <- 1 m 42 s before the restart

  PERM-2 and PERM-3 are generated AFTER the configuration was saved. If they survive the
  restart, then what survived is the LOG being committed to non-volatile storage as the
  event happens -- not the side effect of a `write` that saved the configuration. That
  distinction is the difference between measuring the feature and measuring `write`.

CONFIRMED STORED BEFORE THE RESTART (so that a later absence could not be ambiguous):

  awplus# show log permanent | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:16 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-1
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:20 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-2
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:37 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log permanent | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:40:41 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:41:33 user.notice awplus IMISH[3404]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-3
  (five entries: the three markers and the two read commands)

  STEP 2 VERDICT: PASS -- with the filter raised, notice-level events are stored, and
  three markers were confirmed in the permanent log before anything was interrupted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. STEP 3 -- WHERE IT IS ACTUALLY STORED. The CLI surface is not the device's surface.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"Non-volatile storage" is a claim about MEDIA, and no `show` command on this device names
the media. It was read directly, with tools/awshell.py (a `start-shell` root shell on the
DUT; read-only commands only, nothing written). `start-shell` is logged on the device
(`IMISH[...]: [manager@ttyS0]start-shell`), so the session is auditable, and it is said
here plainly: this case used a root shell on the DUT to read, and wrote nothing in it.

  [root@awplus ~]# mount | grep -E " /flash | /var/log "
  none on /var/log type tmpfs (rw,relatime,size=10240k)
  /dev/mmcblk0p1 on /flash type ext3 (rw,nosuid,nodev,relatime)

  [root@awplus ~]# ls -l /flash/log/
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root          5957 Oct 22 00:41 messages
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         10546 Oct 22 00:24 messages.1
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         11578 Oct 21 23:30 messages.2
  -rw-r--r--    1 root     root         11862 Oct 21 04:29 messages.3

  [root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-PERM /flash/log/*
  /flash/log/messages:4
  /flash/log/messages.1:0
  /flash/log/messages.2:0
  /flash/log/messages.3:0

>>> MEASURED FACT M-3 -- the answer to "what is NVS on this platform", named:
>>>   the VOLATILE log is /var/log/messages, on a **tmpfs** -- a RAM filesystem, 10240k;
>>>   the PERMANENT log is /flash/log/messages, on **/dev/mmcblk0p1, an ext3 filesystem
>>>   on the eMMC device**.
>>> Two different filesystems on two different kinds of media, and the markers were
>>> already in the flash copy BEFORE the restart. The rotation timestamps of
>>> messages.1/.2/.3 (Oct 22 00:24, Oct 21 23:30, Oct 21 04:29) line up with earlier
>>> reboots, so the permanent log rotates at boot and keeps roughly a day of history --
>>> which is why `show log permanent` could show entries from 2026 Oct 21 02:41 at the
>>> start of this case, from before several restarts.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. STEP 4 -- INTERRUPTING THE DEVICE. The power cut was ATTEMPTED and could not be done.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The hardest version of this test is an ABRUPT loss of power, with no graceful shutdown
and no chance for the device to flush anything. That was tried first.

  box: tools/tb ckcon ... 'show clock | include CKT33402-PERM-3'    13:41:34 NZDT
  box: tools/tb pdu tb277 10.36.230.212 1 cycle                     13:41:36 NZDT  (exit 0)
  box: tools/tb pdu tb277 10.36.230.212 1 status  ->  outlet 1 on: True

  The leg answered 4/4 on the FIRST poll, 30 seconds later -- faster than this platform
  can boot (its `reload` takes 60 s just to reach a login prompt). A login prompt is not
  readiness, and neither is a ping: the device was checked instead of assumed.

  awplus# show system | include Uptime
  Uptime             : 0 days 00:18:31

  13:42:05 minus 00:18:31 = 13:23:34 -- exactly T33401's reload. **The DUT never lost
  power and never rebooted.** `show reboot history` gained no entry.

  awplus# show system environment | include PSU|Power
  Resource ID: 1  Name: PSU Bay 1 (AT-PWRSYS4)
  3   PSU Power Output      Yes   -   -   Ok
  4   PSU Power Input       Yes   -   -   Ok
  Resource ID: 2  Name: PSU Bay 2 (AT-PWRSYS4)
  3   PSU Power Output      Yes   -   -   Ok

>>> BENCH FINDING B-1, sent to the Test Engineer as a NEEDS TEST ENGINEER: line and NOT
>>> worked around. **swi_d has TWO power supplies and the session's single PDU outlet
>>> cannot power-cycle it.** The session facts give `outlets: u2=1` on 10.36.230.212, and
>>> that outlet is real -- the PDU accepted the cycle and reports it on -- but the second
>>> AT-PWRSYS4 carried the unit straight through the cut. Nothing was harmed: both bays
>>> read Ok afterwards, Environment Status Normal, no reboot, no log entry.
>>> CONSEQUENCES: (a) this case cannot be given a true AC-interruption test on this bench
>>> as configured, and says so rather than implying it did; (b) more generally, the
>>> FRAMEWORK'S post-failure power cycle would not actually power-cycle swi_d on this
>>> bench either, which matters for any future campaign here.
>>> The second PSU's outlet was NOT hunted for: an outlet outside the session facts may
>>> belong to another unit, and that is STANDING-ORDERS §4. It was asked for, and the
>>> case carried on.

SO THE FALLBACK WAS USED -- `reload` -- and it is still a sound test of this feature,
for a reason that is measured and not assumed: the volatile log lives on a **tmpfs**
(section 7), a RAM filesystem, and a `reload` on this platform is a full kernel restart.
The boot's own kernel lines are in the health-after sample. RAM does not survive it.

  box: tools/tb ckreload tb277 oper1022 u2 115200 600
    13:43:18 >>> reload
    reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
    13:44:18 login seen after 60 s
  (exit 0)

  🔴 A LOGIN PROMPT IS NOT READINESS -- the leg was polled until it answered:
    poll 1  4 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss
    poll 2  4 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss
    poll 3  4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss    <- 13:44:30 NZDT

  STEP 4 VERDICT: PARTIAL AS DESIGNED -- the restart happened, but by `reload`, not by a
  power interruption. The stronger test is declared NOT MEASURABLE on this bench and the
  reason is bench-level, not product-level.

