  🔑 PERM-2 (00:40:20) and PERM-3 (00:41:33) were both generated AFTER the `write` at
  00:40:19. They survived anyway. So what survived is the log being committed to
  non-volatile storage AS THE EVENT HAPPENS, not a side effect of saving the
  configuration.

9b. THE BUFFERED LOG LOST ALL OF THEM -- and this is what makes "NVS" mean something

  awplus# show log | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:44:47 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show log permanent | include CKT33402-PERM
  2026 Oct 22 00:44:51 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-PERM

  Only the two reads just issued. Not one of the five pre-restart entries. Same device,
  same instant, same marker string, two commands: the permanent log has them, the
  buffered log does not.

9c. THE FILTER DECISION SURVIVED TOO

  awplus# show log permanent | include CKT33402-NOTICE
  2026 Oct 22 00:35:37 user.err awplus IMISH[3404]: Command [show log buffered | include CKT33402-NOTICE] failed
  2026 Oct 22 00:44:55 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show log permanent | include CKT33402-NOTICE

  The step-1 notice-level marker `CKT33402-NOTICE-1` is STILL ABSENT after the restart --
  it was never stored and it did not reappear. What IS there from before the restart is
  the `user.err` line of 00:35:37, severity 3, which passed the `warnings` filter when it
  was raised. The permanent log did not keep "everything from before the reboot"; it kept
  exactly what its filter had accepted, and that decision is as durable as the messages.

9d. THE CONFIGURATION SURVIVED

  awplus# show running-config | include log   ->  log permanent level informational
                                                  aaa authentication login default local
  awplus# show startup-config | include log   ->  identical

