STEP 7 -- the restored filter re-proved on a fresh event, not assumed from the absence of
an error:

  awplus# show clock | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
  awplus# show log | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
  2026 Oct 22 00:47:18 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show clock | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE
  2026 Oct 22 00:47:23 user.notice awplus IMISH[2880]: [manager@ttyS0]show log | include CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE

  [root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE /var/log/messages /var/log/messages.1
  /var/log/messages:3
  /var/log/messages.1:0
  [root@awplus ~]# grep -c CKT33402-AFTER-RESTORE /flash/log/messages /flash/log/messages.1
  /flash/log/messages:0
  /flash/log/messages.1:0

  Three occurrences in the volatile log, ZERO on flash. With the filter back at
  `warnings`, a notice is once again not stored. The device is in the state it started in,
  and that is shown rather than asserted.

>>> METHOD FINDING MF-1, which cost this case thirteen minutes and would cost the next
>>> person the same. **`show log permanent | include <pattern>` NEVER RETURNS A PROMPT
>>> when the pattern matches nothing.** It prints no output and the console driver waits
>>> out its whole per-command timeout. It happened twice, and both times the wait equalled
>>> the timeout exactly, which is what identifies it:
>>>   CKTO=180 -> the command sat from 13:36:25 to 13:39:25, exactly 180 s
>>>   CKTO=600 -> the command sat from 13:47:24 to 13:57:24, exactly 600 s, and the DUT's
>>>               own 10-minute console idle-timeout then fired:
>>>               `00:57:20 awplus IMISH[2880]: Console connection timed out.` and the
>>>               console dropped to `awplus login:`
>>> With at least one match it returns promptly (13:40:38 -> 13:40:42, four seconds).
>>> CONSEQUENCE: do not use `show log permanent | include X` to prove an ABSENCE. Prove it
>>> with `grep -c X /flash/log/messages*` in a `start-shell`, which returns a definite 0.
>>> That is how STEP 1's and STEP 7's absences are proved in this log.
>>> The driver's own behaviour was correct throughout: after the device logged the console
>>> out, tools/ printed `cleanup: console busy or not at exec -- sent nothing`, which is
>>> the driver REFUSING to type at a login prompt. That is the designed behaviour, not a
>>> fault.

