reasoning in full -- the single new reboot entry is this case's own permitted reload,
matched to the second, and every flagged line is that boot's ordinary kern.* start-up
text.)

VERDICT: PASS -- log messages written before a restart are stored in non-volatile storage
and are readable afterwards with nothing typed, while the identical entries are gone from
the volatile buffered log; the storage is named and shown to be ext3 on the eMMC while the
volatile log is a tmpfs; markers generated AFTER `write` survived too, so what persists is
the log being committed as events happen and not a side effect of saving the configuration;
the permanent log's filter governs what is stored, proved both before and after the
restart and re-proved after the filter was put back. Graded on a `reload` rather than an
abrupt power interruption: that stronger test was attempted and is NOT MEASURABLE on this
bench because swi_d has two power supplies and the session holds one outlet (bench finding
B-1, reported to the Test Engineer). That limitation is a property of the bench, not of
the product, and it does not affect the step verdicts above.
